This is a fork of https://github.com/ParBLiSS/parconnect, which removes most benchmarking code to provide a reusable library just containing the actual algorithm and its dependencies.

This also tries to fix some bugs and modernize the build process.

de Bruijn graph generator
-------------------------

`lib/graphGen/deBruijn/deBruijnGraphGen.hpp` is upstream's de Bruijn graph
generator: it builds the k=31 de Bruijn graph of a DNA read file with BLISS and
returns it as a distributed edge list. It is kept out of the `parconnect` target
because BLISS is a heavy dependency that nothing else needs -- enable
`PARCONNECT_ENABLE_DEBRUIJN` and link `parconnect::debruijn` instead.

`tools/dbg2parhip` wraps it into a converter, replacing upstream's
`utils_exportBinaryFormat` (which wrote one ad-hoc binary file per rank):

    mpirun -n <p> ./tools/dbg2parhip reads.fastq graph.parhip

The input needs a `.fastq` or `.fasta` extension -- BLISS dispatches its parser
on that -- and must be uncompressed, with the DNA alphabet restricted to
{A,C,G,T}. The output is a single ParHiP graph, so any KaGen consumer can read
it back with `file;filename=graph.parhip;input_format=parhip`.

The tool compacts the packed-31-mer vertex ids to [0, n) (upstream's
`reduceVertexIds`), repartitions the edges so that each rank owns a contiguous
vertex block with all of its edges, and drops duplicate and self edges. The
result does not depend on the number of ranks: the output file is byte-identical
for any `p`.

Both options default to ON for a top-level build and OFF when this project is
consumed via `FetchContent`, so a consumer of the connectivity algorithm alone
pulls in neither BLISS nor KaGen.
